module gunbc.runner.runner_host_surface

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr, String }
import gunbc.build_cache_instance { ProcessIdentity }

// THE RUNTIME SURFACE IS DERIVED, NOT DISCOVERED. The invariant this module exists to make
// constructible: no user-space process, listener, route, executable, writable path, device grant or
// secret ingress exists on the runner host unless one modeled declaration here caused it. A red team
// should be able to enumerate the intended surface WITHOUT TOUCHING THE UNIT, because the same
// values that build and launch the machine are the ones that answer the question.
//
// A SCAN IS THE THIRD LAYER, NEVER THE FIRST. Three questions with three different authorities:
//
//   DECLARED   what processes and interfaces MAY exist          this module
//   ENFORCED   what prevents anything else existing             /init, namespaces, filter, image
//   OBSERVED   does THIS boot match the declaration exactly     whole-population runtime receipt
//
// A declaration alone is prose with better syntax. A scan alone describes whatever happened to
// start. The standing is the conjunction, and this module is deliberately only the first term.
//
// THE PROCESS IS THE ROOT, NOT THE PORT. A port is one projection of a running process, and a
// surface described as a port list cannot answer who owns the socket, what that owner may execute,
// what it may write, or what it may reach outbound. Modelling ports first is how a "surface
// document" becomes a fourth authority that happens to agree with sshd_config, the firewall and the
// probe -- which is four authorities, not one, and they agree only until they do not.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT YET CARRY, named so its absence is not read as a claim:
// capability sets, seccomp policy digests, mount grants, device grants and secret ingress are axes
// of RuntimeProcessClass in the ruling this follows, and are NOT modeled here. This slice models the
// process/listener/lineage spine and the exact-population judgment over it. An unmodeled axis is an
// axis nothing enforces, and the surface report must say so rather than imply completeness.

// CARDINALITY IS PART OF THE DECLARATION BECAUSE PIDS CANNOT BE. Nothing can name the pid a
// supervisor will get, so the declared population is a set of CLASSES with counts -- some fixed,
// some keyed by an admitted attempt, and one that is the whole point: Forbidden.
//
// ForbiddenClass IS NOT A COMMENT. It is the arm that makes "anything else" a representable,
// checkable state rather than the silent complement of a list.
type ProcessCardinality
  = ExactlyOne
  | ZeroOrMorePerControlSession
  | ExactlyOnePerAdmittedAttempt
  | ForbiddenClass

// THE ROLES OF THE RAW RUNNER HOST, and the smallness is the design rather than an accident of this
// slice. In production the static user-space graph is a supervisor and a control ingress; everything
// else exists only because an attempt was admitted, and disappears when it retires.
//
// THERE IS NO IDLE FIRECRACKER. A hypervisor process with no admitted attempt behind it is not a
// warm pool, it is an unexplained process, and the population judgment treats it as one.
// ONE ATTEMPT EXECUTION IDENTITY, NOT TWO PROCESSES. The roster used to name AttemptJailer and
// AttemptFirecracker as separate classes with the second parented to the first, and a live census
// disproved it directly: the pid launched as the jailer and the pid running Firecracker were the
// SAME NUMBER (1649), with PPid 1. The jailer prepares and then execs, so the executable and the
// role change while the process identity does not.
//
// FIXING ONLY THE COUNT LEFT THE MODEL SAYING TWO THINGS AT ONCE -- the arithmetic said one process
// while this roster still declared two with a parent-child edge, so a consumer's answer depended on
// which half it read. The roster is the authority a census compares against, so it is the half that
// had to change.
//
// THE DEADLINE WATCHER IS A ROLE NOW BECAUSE IT IS A PROCESS. BusyBox timeout forks a watcher that
// outlives its exec, and the same census saw it: pid 1651, busybox, uid 0, parented to init. It was
// previously counted and unnamed, which is precisely the "exists only as a number" shape this
// module rejects everywhere else.
type HostProcessRole
  = HostSupervisor
  | ControlMaster
  | ControlSession
  | AttemptExecution
  | AttemptDeadlineWatcher
  | RuntimeSurfaceObserver

fn host_process_role_label(r: HostProcessRole) -> String {
  match r {
    HostSupervisor => "host-supervisor"
    ControlMaster => "control-master"
    ControlSession => "control-session"
    AttemptExecution => "attempt-execution"
    AttemptDeadlineWatcher => "attempt-deadline-watcher"
    RuntimeSurfaceObserver => "runtime-surface-observer"
  }
}

// THE PHASE IS WHAT CHANGES, and it is modeled beside the role rather than as two roles. A snapshot
// taken after the guest marker should find FirecrackerRunning; finding JailerPreparing there would
// mean the exec never happened, which is a different failure from the process being absent.
type AttemptExecutionPhase
  = JailerPreparing
  | FirecrackerRunning
  | AttemptExecutionRetiring

fn attempt_phase_label(p: AttemptExecutionPhase) -> String {
  match p {
    JailerPreparing => "jailer-preparing"
    FirecrackerRunning => "firecracker-running"
    AttemptExecutionRetiring => "attempt-execution-retiring"
  }
}

// LINEAGE IS DECLARED, NOT INFERRED FROM WHATEVER RAN. "A firecracker exists" and "a firecracker
// exists BENEATH THE JAILER THAT WAS ADMITTED FOR THIS ATTEMPT" are different facts, and only the
// second is the isolation the jailer is there to provide. A firecracker parented anywhere else has
// escaped its jail by construction, whatever its command line says.
type ProcessParent
  = ParentIsKernel
  | ParentIsRole { role: HostProcessRole }

type HostProcessClass {
  role: HostProcessRole
  cardinality: ProcessCardinality
  parent: ProcessParent
  executable_digest: NonEmptyStr
  runs_as: NonEmptyStr
  may_spawn: List<HostProcessRole>
}

// ── the listener, at its real identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A LISTENER IS NOT A PORT NUMBER. These are four materially different surfaces and a model that
// records "22/tcp" cannot tell them apart:
//
//   127.0.0.1:22/tcp      reachable by nothing off-box
//   192.168.1.230:22/tcp  reachable from the management network
//   0.0.0.0:22/tcp        reachable from every attached network
//   [::]:22/tcp           the same mistake, in a family people forget to check
//
// The namespace matters for the same reason: an endpoint inside an attempt's network namespace is
// not on the host's surface at all, and conflating the two would report guest-side listeners as host
// exposure or, far worse, the reverse.
type AddressFamily = Inet4 | Inet6 | UnixDomain
type TransportProtocol = Tcp | Udp | UnixStream

type NetworkNamespaceScope
  = HostNetworkNamespace
  | AttemptNetworkNamespace

type ExposureZone
  = ManagementNetworkOnly
  | LoopbackOnly
  | HostLocalIpcOnly
  | AnyAttachedNetwork

type DeclaredListener {
  owner: HostProcessRole
  namespace_scope: NetworkNamespaceScope
  family: AddressFamily
  protocol: TransportProtocol
  local_address: NonEmptyStr
  port: Int
  zone: ExposureZone
}

// ── the declared surface of the raw runner host ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE PRODUCTION SURFACE. One supervisor, one control ingress, and nothing standing idle.
//
// THE CONTROL LISTENER IS A TRANSPORT TO ONE PROGRAM, NOT A SHELL. An ordinary SSH login re-opens
// the surface completely: it permits arbitrary combinations of every executable in the image, so the
// process population stops being closed even though the binary set is finite. Public-key only,
// forced command, no pty, no agent or TCP forwarding, management address only -- the caller reaches
// the host control agent and cannot reach anything else.
//
// THIS IS ALSO WHY THE FLEET'S ORDINARY SSH REALIZATION DOES NOT FIT HERE: it invokes `bash -s`,
// which both requires a shell this image should not contain and hands it arbitrary script text. The
// stateless host needs a typed control surface, not a generic exec transport.
data runner_host_production_processes: List<HostProcessClass> = [
  HostProcessClass {
    role: HostSupervisor,
    cardinality: ExactlyOne,
    parent: ParentIsKernel,
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root",
    may_spawn: [ControlMaster, AttemptExecution, AttemptDeadlineWatcher],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: ControlMaster,
    cardinality: ExactlyOne,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: HostSupervisor },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root",
    may_spawn: [ControlSession],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: ControlSession,
    cardinality: ZeroOrMorePerControlSession,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: ControlMaster },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "gunbc-control",
    may_spawn: [],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: AttemptExecution,
    cardinality: ExactlyOnePerAdmittedAttempt,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: HostSupervisor },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root during the jailer phase, then the attempt uid after the drop -- one identity, two executables",
    may_spawn: [],
  },
]

// FIRECRACKER EXPOSES NO HOST LISTENER. Its API is either absent entirely, when the whole
// configuration is known before start, or an attempt-scoped Unix socket when lifecycle control needs
// it. Either way it is not a TCP endpoint on the host's surface, and a firecracker holding one is a
// finding rather than a configuration choice.
data runner_host_production_listeners: List<DeclaredListener> = [
  DeclaredListener {
    owner: ControlMaster,
    namespace_scope: HostNetworkNamespace,
    family: Inet4,
    protocol: Tcp,
    local_address: "management-address",
    port: 22,
    zone: ManagementNetworkOnly,
  },
]

// ── production and recovery are mutually exclusive ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// A RECOVERY SHELL IN THE IMAGE IS NOT A RECOVERY SHELL ON THE SURFACE. Carrying busybox so a
// console operator can diagnose a unit does not mean a network caller may reach it, and the two get
// conflated precisely because the binary is present either way. Modelling the boot PURPOSE keeps the
// distinction: a host in recovery refuses to admit VM work, and a host admitting VM work has no
// interactive shell reachable.
//
// THE DANGEROUS STATE IS THE UNION, and it is unrepresentable here rather than discouraged: there is
// no arm that is both.
// A THIRD PURPOSE EXISTS BECAUSE A REAL BOOT HAD NO TRUTHFUL CONSTRUCTOR HERE. The 2026-09-06 KVM
// probe ran Firecracker DIRECTLY, with no jailer -- which the production graph forbids, since it
// requires HostSupervisor -> AttemptJailer -> AttemptFirecracker. And recovery refuses VM work
// outright. So the machine that actually ran was representable as neither, and a two-armed model
// would have forced that run to be labelled production simply because it achieved the production
// path's first interesting effect.
//
// WHAT THE QUALIFICATION PURPOSE ADMITS IS DELIBERATELY NARROW: one bounded attempt, one guest
// pinned by digest, no arbitrary or GitHub workload, and direct Firecracker permitted FOR THIS
// MILESTONE ONLY. It is not a weaker production; it is a different subject, and nothing running
// under it may be described as production-ready.
type HostBootPurpose
  = RunnerHostProduction
  | RunnerHostQualificationProbe
  | RunnerHostRecovery

fn admits_vm_work(purpose: HostBootPurpose) -> Bool {
  match purpose {
    RunnerHostProduction => true
    RunnerHostQualificationProbe => true
    RunnerHostRecovery => false
  }
}

// ARBITRARY WORKLOAD IS THE DISTINCTION THAT MATTERS, not whether a VM starts. The qualification
// profile runs a known, digest-pinned guest; admitting someone else's code under it would be
// running untrusted work without the containment the production graph exists to require.
fn admits_arbitrary_workload(purpose: HostBootPurpose) -> Bool {
  match purpose {
    RunnerHostProduction => true
    RunnerHostQualificationProbe => false
    RunnerHostRecovery => false
  }
}

// THE JAILER IS REQUIRED BY PRODUCTION AND WAIVED ONLY FOR QUALIFICATION, and the waiver is written
// as its own function so it is a decision someone can find rather than an omission in a launch
// path. Firecracker's own guidance is that production deployments start it through the jailer; a
// probe that skips it proves KVM works and proves nothing about containment.
fn requires_jailer(purpose: HostBootPurpose) -> Bool {
  match purpose {
    RunnerHostProduction => true
    RunnerHostQualificationProbe => false
    RunnerHostRecovery => false
  }
}

fn admits_interactive_console_shell(purpose: HostBootPurpose) -> Bool {
  match purpose {
    RunnerHostProduction => false
    RunnerHostQualificationProbe => true
    RunnerHostRecovery => true
  }
}

fn is_production_ready(purpose: HostBootPurpose) -> Bool {
  match purpose {
    RunnerHostProduction => true
    RunnerHostQualificationProbe => false
    RunnerHostRecovery => false
  }
}

// ── the exact-population judgment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE EXPECTED POPULATION IS DERIVED FROM THE ADMITTED WORK, not fixed. Static singletons plus one
// jailer and one firecracker per admitted attempt plus the live control sessions -- so the same
// declaration answers "what should be running" at any moment without anyone maintaining a second
// list that drifts.
// THE JAILER AND FIRECRACKER ARE NOT AUTOMATICALLY TWO PROCESSES, and the declaration above said
// they were. A live run on 2026-09-06 disproved it: the stock v1.16.1 jailer performs its
// privileged setup -- cgroup, mount namespace, pivot, device creation, uid/gid drop -- and then
// EXECS INTO the Firecracker binary. Without --new-pid-ns those are two PHASES OF ONE PROCESS
// IDENTITY, not a parent and a child, so an expected population of two per attempt would be wrong
// by one on every attempt and a census would report a missing process that never existed.
//
// THE TOPOLOGY IS A PROPERTY OF THE INVOCATION, so it is carried rather than assumed. --new-pid-ns
// makes the jailer clone a child and the two-process shape becomes correct; a daemonizing
// realization changes it again. A model that hardcodes either shape is wrong for the other.
//
// AND IT HAS A CONSEQUENCE FOR CLEANUP EVIDENCE. Under in-place exec, "zero surviving jailer" is
// NOT an independent fact about teardown: the jailer stopped existing as a jailer at the moment it
// became Firecracker, whether or not anything was cleaned up. The postcondition that carries weight
// is that no process identity ASSOCIATED WITH THE ATTEMPT remains -- the deadline watcher and the
// execution identity included.
// THE TWO JAILER OPTIONS ARE ORTHOGONAL BOOLEANS, NOT ALTERNATIVES. --new-pid-ns and --daemonize
// are independent fields in the jailer, so a coproduct of "in place OR new-pid-ns OR daemonized"
// makes mutually exclusive what the invocation does not. They are two axes.
//
// AND NEITHER LEAVES TWO STEADY-STATE PROCESSES. With --new-pid-ns the jailer clones and the parent
// exits; with --daemonize the parent and an intermediate exit and the daemon continues. Both have
// transient extra identities during launch and ONE Firecracker execution process after settling.
// What differs between them is which pid becomes Firecracker, whether a pid namespace exists,
// whether the process is session-detached, and what parent relationship survives -- none of which
// is a steady-state count.
type JailerPidNamespace = JailerSharedPidNamespace | JailerNewPidNamespace
type JailerSession = JailerForeground | JailerDaemonized

type JailerInvocationTopology {
  pid_namespace: JailerPidNamespace
  session: JailerSession
}

// THE DEADLINE MECHANISM AND THE JAILER TOPOLOGY ARE NOT INDEPENDENT, and treating them as two
// separate count terms hid a correctness rule. An external watcher owns the lifetime of the pid it
// was given -- which survives an in-place exec, so the watched pid becomes Firecracker and the
// deadline still binds. It does NOT survive a fork: under --new-pid-ns the watched parent exits and
// Firecracker continues as a child, and under --daemonize the watched process exits during
// daemonization. In both cases the watcher would fire against a dead pid while the VM ran on.
//
// SO THE COMBINATION IS ADMITTED OR REFUSED, never merely counted. The production direction removes
// the question: a supervisor holding a pidfd and enforcing its own deadline has no watcher process
// and nothing to rebind after a fork.
type DeadlineRealization
  = DeadlineByExternalWatcher
  | DeadlineBySupervisorInternal

type DeadlineTopologyAdmission
  = DeadlineBindingHolds
  | DeadlineBindingLostOnFork { detail: String }

fn deadline_topology_admission(
  deadline: DeadlineRealization,
  topology: JailerInvocationTopology,
) -> DeadlineTopologyAdmission {
  match deadline {
    DeadlineBySupervisorInternal => DeadlineBindingHolds
    DeadlineByExternalWatcher =>
      match topology.pid_namespace {
        JailerNewPidNamespace =>
          DeadlineBindingLostOnFork { detail: "--new-pid-ns makes the jailer clone a child and the watched parent exit, so the watcher would fire against a dead pid while the VM continues" }
        JailerSharedPidNamespace =>
          match topology.session {
            JailerDaemonized =>
              DeadlineBindingLostOnFork { detail: "--daemonize makes the watched process exit during daemonization while the daemon continues" }
            JailerForeground => DeadlineBindingHolds
          }
      }
  }
}

fn deadline_watcher_processes(realization: DeadlineRealization) -> Int {
  match realization {
    DeadlineByExternalWatcher => 1
    DeadlineBySupervisorInternal => 0
  }
}

// AN UNRESOLVED EXPECTATION REFUSES RATHER THAN RETURNING ZERO. The previous form answered zero
// processes for an unobserved topology, which is fail-open in the worst direction: for an ADMITTED
// attempt, not knowing the topology does not establish that no attempt process should exist, and a
// census comparing against zero would report every real process as unexpected -- or, worse, find
// nothing and call it agreement.
type AttemptProcessExpectation
  = AttemptProcessExpectationResolved { processes: Int }
  | AttemptProcessExpectationUnresolved { cause: String }

fn attempt_process_expectation(
  topology: JailerInvocationTopology,
  deadline: DeadlineRealization,
) -> AttemptProcessExpectation {
  match deadline_topology_admission(deadline: deadline, topology: topology) {
    DeadlineBindingLostOnFork { detail: d } =>
      AttemptProcessExpectationUnresolved { cause: concat("the deadline mechanism does not bind under this topology: ", d) }
    DeadlineBindingHolds =>
      AttemptProcessExpectationResolved {
        processes: 1 + deadline_watcher_processes(realization: deadline),
      }
  }
}

fn expected_process_count(
  admitted_attempts: Int,
  control_sessions: Int,
  topology: JailerInvocationTopology,
  deadline: DeadlineRealization,
) -> AttemptProcessExpectation {
  match attempt_process_expectation(topology: topology, deadline: deadline) {
    AttemptProcessExpectationUnresolved { cause: c } => AttemptProcessExpectationUnresolved { cause: c }
    AttemptProcessExpectationResolved { processes: per } =>
      AttemptProcessExpectationResolved { processes: 2 + control_sessions + (per * admitted_attempts) }
  }
}

type ObservedProcess {
  identity: ProcessIdentity
  role_claimed: HostProcessRole
  parent_identity: ProcessIdentity
  executable_digest: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE FAILURES ARE TYPED AND EACH NAMES A DIFFERENT REMEDY. A single Bool here would be the
// typed-cause-erased failure this repository keeps finding: "the surface does not match" sends
// nobody anywhere, while "a firecracker is parented outside its jailer" and "sshd is listening on
// 0.0.0.0 instead of the management address" are different incidents with different owners.
//
// RuntimeSurfaceObservationUnreadable IS THE ARM THAT KEEPS THE OTHERS HONEST. An observation that
// could not be taken is not an observation of nothing, and folding it into "exact" would make a
// failed census the most reassuring outcome available.
type RuntimeSurfaceFinding
  = UnexpectedProcess { observed: ProcessIdentity, executable_digest: NonEmptyStr }
  | MissingExpectedProcess { role: HostProcessRole }
  | UnexpectedChildLineage { role: HostProcessRole, expected_parent: HostProcessRole }
  | ExecutableIdentityMismatch { role: HostProcessRole, expected: NonEmptyStr, observed: NonEmptyStr }
  | UnexpectedListener { local_address: NonEmptyStr, port: Int }
  | MissingExpectedListener { owner: HostProcessRole, port: Int }
  | ListenerOwnedByWrongProcess { port: Int, expected_owner: HostProcessRole }
  | ListenerInWrongNamespace { port: Int }
  | UnexpectedEgressRoute { destination: NonEmptyStr }
  | FirewallPolicyMismatch { detail: String }
  | RuntimeSurfaceObservationUnreadable { detail: String }

type RuntimeSurfaceStanding
  = RuntimeSurfaceHeld
  | RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: List<RuntimeSurfaceFinding> }

// COUNTS ARE NOT PROOF, and this function exists to say so in a place the compiler can see. Three
// expected and three observed is satisfied by three substitutions. The standing is an IDENTITY JOIN
// over roles, lineage and executable digests; the count is a cheap precondition that can only ever
// produce a finding, never discharge one.
// AN EMPTY FINDINGS LIST IS NOT AN OBSERVATION, and the previous form let a caller pass one and
// receive RuntimeSurfaceHeld without having looked at a single process. That is the same fail-open
// shape as an unobserved topology answering zero: absence of findings from an absent census is not
// agreement.
//
// SO THE STANDING TAKES A COMPLETENESS WITNESS AS WELL. A snapshot that could not be taken, or that
// raced the population it was enumerating, refuses -- and refusing is the whole point, because a
// runner host that cannot account for itself must not admit work.
type SnapshotCompleteness
  = SnapshotComplete { processes_enumerated: Int }
  | SnapshotRaced { detail: String }
  | SnapshotUnreadable { detail: String }

fn runtime_surface_standing(
  completeness: SnapshotCompleteness,
  findings: List<RuntimeSurfaceFinding>,
) -> RuntimeSurfaceStanding {
  match completeness {
    SnapshotUnreadable { detail: d } =>
      RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: [RuntimeSurfaceObservationUnreadable { detail: d }] }
    SnapshotRaced { detail: d } =>
      RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: [RuntimeSurfaceObservationUnreadable { detail: concat("the snapshot raced the process population it was enumerating: ", d) }] }
    SnapshotComplete { processes_enumerated: _ } =>
      if (findings |> count) == 0 {
        RuntimeSurfaceHeld
      } else {
        RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: findings }
      }
  }
}

// A HOST THAT CANNOT ACCOUNT FOR ITSELF DOES NOT TAKE NEW WORK. An unexpected listener is not
// dashboard colour: it is an unexplained process holding a socket on a machine about to be handed
// someone else's code to run. Admission is gated on the standing rather than reported beside it.
fn admits_new_attempt(purpose: HostBootPurpose, standing: RuntimeSurfaceStanding) -> Bool {
  match standing {
    RuntimeSurfaceHeld => admits_vm_work(purpose: purpose)
    RuntimeSurfaceViolated { findings: _ } => false
  }
}

// ── the boundary of the claim ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THREE POPULATIONS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS CLAIMED EXACTLY. Stating this is what keeps the
// invariant honest rather than grandiose.
//
//   HOST USER-SPACE   exactly modeled, exactly observed -- the subject of this module
//   KERNEL SURFACE    config, built-in drivers, protocol families, syscall and LSM policy. Modeled
//                     separately because kernel threads are not ordinary processes and their
//                     population varies with hardware and activity.
//   GUEST WORKLOAD    deliberately arbitrary. An Actions job runs whatever it runs.
//
// WHAT IS CLOSED FOR THE GUEST IS ITS ENVELOPE, NOT ITS PROCESS LIST: no host namespace, no host
// mounts, no host devices beyond virtual ones, no inbound route, default-deny egress, one
// attempt-scoped credential, a dedicated kernel, bounded memory, cpu and disk. Those are the axes
// gunbc.product.fabric.isolation already names, and this module consumes that vocabulary rather than
// restating it.
type SurfacePopulation
  = HostUserSpaceExactlyModeled
  | KernelSurfaceModeledSeparately
  | GuestWorkloadArbitraryWithinEnvelope

// THE BMC IS OBSERVED AND MUST NEVER BE DERIVED. We do not build or launch its processes, so its
// surface cannot honestly come from our image -- it comes from an exact controller, an exact
// firmware identity, and an observed listener population. Presenting it beside the derived host
// surface as though both had the same provenance is the same portability error the MegaRAC lane is
// currently correcting: an observed property of one vendor build reported as a constructed property.
//
// So the asset report a red team receives is a COMPOSITION with its provenance visible per part,
// never one flat list.
type SurfaceProvenance
  = DerivedFromOurImage
  | ObservedFromVendorFirmware
  | ObservedFromNetworkFabric

// THE QUALIFICATION PROCESS GRAPH, declared beside the production one rather than derived from it
// by subtraction. It is what the 2026-09-06 probe actually ran: a supervisor, and one Firecracker
// launched directly for one named attempt. The production roster above is unchanged and still
// requires the jailer between them.
data runner_host_qualification_processes: List<HostProcessClass> = [
  HostProcessClass {
    role: HostSupervisor,
    cardinality: ExactlyOne,
    parent: ParentIsKernel,
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root",
    may_spawn: [AttemptExecution],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: AttemptExecution,
    cardinality: ExactlyOnePerAdmittedAttempt,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: HostSupervisor },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "uid 1000 after the jailer's privilege drop; the same process identity that began as the jailer, measured as pid 1649 in both phases",
    may_spawn: [],
  },
  HostProcessClass {
    role: AttemptDeadlineWatcher,
    cardinality: ExactlyOnePerAdmittedAttempt,
    parent: ParentIsRole { role: HostSupervisor },
    executable_digest: "unbound-until-image-build",
    runs_as: "root",
    may_spawn: [],
  },
]

// THE QUALIFICATION PROFILE DECLARES NO LISTENER AT ALL, which is worth stating rather than leaving
// as an empty list nobody reads. The probe image has no network configured and no sshd; its only
// ingress is the serial console, which is not a network surface and is reachable only through the
// BMC.
data runner_host_qualification_listeners: List<DeclaredListener> = []
